n  if you think about today how important a  high school diploma is at that time for  but child to get a diploma really meant  something and so those families had  worked so hard for that moment that they  that they were you know trying to find  ways that they could that their kids can  graduate and so some students were sent  to live with family members you know  it's just his older sisters or aunts in  the north in particular some students  went and lived at a college in North  Carolina an AME Church related school  that was educating some high school  students agreed to take in about sixty  students from Prince Edward County and  then the black churches also started  these sent training centers in the  basement of their churches where they  were not considered schools and they  they were not taught by you know  official teachers and they were not full  day programs but they were meant to  engage young students so that you know  they could have they could have some  involvement with schooling even though  you know they wouldn't be going to  school that year and so parents of  younger kids did send their their kids  to those training centers  but I have to tell you the vast majority  of black children did not go to school  that year it did not go to school in  subsequent years  nobody had any way of knowing that the  schools would be closed for as long as  they were so children who were old  enough went to work in the fields with  with their parents I mean and and that  extra income I mean most people in  Prince Edward were tobacco farmers and  so the extra income they could get from  extra hands actually meant something to  those families and so it was a positive  in that way but it also meant that when  the schools would reopen many years  later the students were lost they had  been you know they've been working all  those years they they weren't gonna go  back to school and so this generation of  children came to be known as the Lost  Generation because so many 