ere to go through  the back door of the house through the  house out the front door through the  front yard and up the steps of that big  bus and they were never to tell anyone  that they didn't live there because if  they did their education would be at  stake that story just gives me shivers  even today every time I think about you  know what she had to endure to get an  education and what her parents were  willing to sacrifice to make sure that  happened years later that woman Dorothy  Holcomb became a school board member in  Prince Edward County she also worked her  career the state employment office in  Prince Edward County and kids that she  had gone to school with kids she knew  from her neighborhood would come in  seeking unemployment benefits or looking  for jobs and she would have to go to the  other side of her desk and help them  fill out the forms because they were  illiterate  this so this five-year period of not  having schools you know not only  affected those kids and their parents  but it has affected generations of  children in Prince Edward County right  because the illiteracy of those parents  has resulted in in their children not  being as literate as they would have  been otherwise right and I think about  the myriad of other effects of not  having an education that means you you  not not only didn't get to achieve your  dreams in life but the economic  situation that they're in would have  been totally different had they had a  high school diploma had they been able  to go to college right it might have  meant that they could leave that town  and get better jobs it might have meant  they could buy a house so so the impact  on my generation and subsequent  generations has been really significant  and that's part of what I wanted to  explore in this book too I also wanted  to look at what those public schools  look like after they reopened and and  the effect on the town today so that  white Academy that that my grandfather  and other white lea